course: dictionary
lesson: 15254
title: Shoplift: stealing from a shop
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Shoplift is a verb. It means to steal things from a shop by hiding them and leaving without paying. Shoplift, shoplifts, shoplifted and shoplifting all describe the same kind of action, at different times.
explain: Read this sentence: «The security guard saw the boy shoplift a chocolate bar.» The clue is the security guard, because shops watch for stealing. The sentence does not say how the boy did it, so use the context: he took the chocolate bar and did not pay for it. That is shoplifting.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «The boy hid a chocolate bar in his coat and walked out without paying.» Does that still make sense? Yes, so your meaning fits. Shoplifting is a crime, even when the thing taken is small, like a sweet.
fact: Shoplift is a verb: I shoplift, he shoplifts, she shoplifted, they are shoplifting.
fact: «Shoplift» means to steal things from a shop by hiding them and leaving without paying.
fact: Stealing even a sweet is still stealing, so taking a sweet without paying is shoplifting.
fact: Many shops have cameras and security guards to watch for shoplifting.
fact: A sign that says «Shoplifters will be prosecuted» means people who steal from the shop will be taken to court.
exercise: «The security guard saw the boy shoplift a chocolate bar.» What does shoplift mean? | To sell old toys at a market | To lift heavy boxes onto a shelf | To buy something with a gift card | *To steal from a shop by hiding things and leaving without paying
exercise: Copy one word from this sentence that means stealing from a shop: «Lily wanted the toy, but she knew it was wrong to shoplift.» | *shoplift | Lily | toy | wanted
exercise: «Tom paid for his sweets at the till, so he did not shoplift.» What does this show about Tom? | He stole the sweets and ran out | *He did not steal, because he paid for what he took | He forgot his money at home and left | He gave the sweets to a friend
test: «Shoplifting is a crime, and even one sweet counts.» What is the best meaning of shoplifting here? | Counting how many sweets are on a shelf | *Stealing something from a shop and leaving without paying | Giving a sweet to a friend | Buying a big bag of sweets at the shop
test: In a story, a girl feels pushed by her friends and takes a pen from a shop without paying. What might the author be showing? | That the girl is very rich | *That someone can feel under pressure to shoplift | That pens cost nothing at all | That the shop was closed that day
exam: Which sentence shows shoplifting? | *Sam hid a phone case in his bag and walked out without paying | Sam paid at the till and said thank you to the cashier | Sam gave the cashier a five pound note | Sam asked the shop owner if he could have a discount
